From ec7f0a5a2a4b57e28c2c2c48d8ac22564555e7dd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Shamik Saha <shamik.saha.rcciit@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 18:13:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] examples: an accounts-payable agent that overspends its
 budget

A LangGraph agent told to clear overdue supplier invoices, with a EUR 500
daily budget and a EUR 100 per-payment approval threshold. It makes 23
tool calls. Every one is under the threshold. Three configurations run
the identical call sequence:

  A. no gate               EUR 4,367.59
  B. per-call threshold    EUR 1,967.59   every call is under it
  C. onedoor               EUR   462.65   within budget

The demo exists to make three things concrete: a per-call check cannot
see a sequence, because it has no memory to compare against; renaming the
tool is not a second wallet, because both payment tools carry the
`money_out` effect label and share one counter; and an irreversible wire
never auto-executes at any tier, because it has no compensating command.

Run C repeats five times. LangGraph's ToolNode runs tool calls
concurrently, so *which* payments get through varies with scheduling --
EUR 461.84 to EUR 463.04 across runs, cap breached zero times. A budget
that only holds single-threaded is not a budget, so the variance is
reported rather than averaged away.

The call sequence is fixed rather than produced by a live model. That is
a real limitation and the module says so: this shows what a gate does
with a sequence, not how likely a model is to produce one.

This example is what found F7.
